/P 359 0 R
/E (Introduction)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
500 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 359 0 R
/E (   or centuries, physics sought to explain the universe through objective laws, assuming systems have definite properties whether we look at them or not. But quantum mechanics, and its measurement problem, challenged that picture. By exploring key developments like Bell's theorem, Wheeler's thought experiments, and decoherence, this article examines how physics and philosophy intersect. Ultimately, the measurement problem remains unresolved scientifically, yet its philosophical impact is profound. Whether through Copenhagen, Many-Worlds, or QBism, interpretations reflect diverse ways of understanding reality without changing physical predictions. What it shows is that answering fundamental questions about the universe demands both empirical science and philosophical reasoning working together. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
501 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 359 0 R
/E (F)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
502 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 359 0 R
/E (The Classical Belief)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
503 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 359 0 R
/E (In classical physics, scientists operated under the philosophy of scientific realism. Scientific realism is the interpretation that the universe described by science exists independent of perception. Where objects possess definite properties, such as position and velocity, regardless of whether they are observed. An objective reality is the older belief that systems exist independent to human awareness, was therefore pivotal to classical physics. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
