517 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 362 0 R
/E (Could Decoherence Fix the Measurement Problem? )
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
518 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 362 0 R
/E (With respect to the measurement problem, decoherence provides an explanation for the transition of a system to an observable state. But decoherence does not claim to provide a mechanism for the actual wave-function collapse; instead, it puts forward a framework for the appearance of the wave-function collapse. If a system is not perfectly isolated, for example, during a measurement, coherence is shared with the environment and appears to be lost over time. Quantum coherence is not lost but mixed with many more degrees of freedom in the environment. This is analogous to friction in classical mechanics, where energy is transferred as heat to the environment rather than disappearing entirely. Although decoherence explains why superpositions become unobservable in everyday systems, it does not explain why we experience a single outcome, which gives both physics and philosophy a chance to keep working on this issue in quantum mechanics. )
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
