520 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 363 0 R
/E (Quantum mechanics has left scientists unsure of a single theory about the nature of reality, leading to several key interpretations: )
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
521 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1585 0 R
/E (The Copenhagen Interpretation is a leading theory brought forward mainly by Bohr and Heisenberg. It attributes the indefinite state of a particle prior to observation to a fundamental limit on human knowledge. )
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
522 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1586 0 R
/E (Hugh Everett proposed the Many-Worlds Interpretation, saying that the wave function never collapses but instead evolves continuously everywhere. Every outcome that is possible for the quantum event does happen as the universe splits into multiple parallel, non-communicating realities. Regarding philosophy this interpretation changes ideas about the nature of probability and identity across branches of the universe. )
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
523 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1587 0 R
/E (Quantum Bayesianism \(QBism\) merges Bayesian probability with quantum mechanics. Many aspects of quantum formalism are subjective in nature. For example, a quantum state is not an element of reality, representing degrees of belief an agent has about the possible outcomes of measurements. Philosophers of science deem this interpretation a form of anti-realism, the founders call QBism participatory realism which is when reality consists of more than can be captured by a third person account. )
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
524 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 363 0 R
/T (Conclusion)
/E (Conclusion)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
525 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 363 0 R
/E (The measurement problem remains one of the greatest unresolved questions in modern science, sitting right at the crossroads of physics and philosophy. While experiments like those testing Bell's theorem rule out simple classical views of reality, they do not tell us which interpretation of quantum mechanics is correct. That is where philosophy steps in to evaluate what those physical facts really mean. In the end, the profound takeaway may be that neither discipline alone can give the full picture. The ongoing conversation between science and philosophy that edges us closer to understanding what reality really is. )
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
