/E (   f you were asked to name the most abundant acid on Earth, what would you think of? Citric acid might come to mind - its presence in many citrus fruits as well as in cleaning products and even pharmaceuticals might make up a large volume worldwide. Another suggestion might be lactic acid, the acid that every human being produces when their cells break down glucose for energy. Surely the cells in all 8.3 billion of us produce enough acids to dominate everything else?)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
561 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 370 0 R
/E (What if we flipped the question, asking instead what the most abundant base on Earth is? Ammonia might be a common answer due to its significance in agricultural fertilizers, cleaning products and manufacturing processes. Substances such as calcium hydroxide and sodium bicarbonate \(known commonly as baking soda\) are bases that appear in toothpaste, helping to protect tooth enamel from decay by neutralising acids produced by bacteria.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
562 0 obj
<<
