564 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 370 0 R
/E (According to Bronsted-Lowry definitions, an acid is a substance that donates a proton \(also known as a H+ ion\) to another species during a reaction. Conversely, a base is a substance that accepts a proton from another species. We can see this process more clearly when looking at the example of the reaction between hydrochloric acid \(HCl\) and sodium hydroxide \(OH-\). HCl donates a H+ ion to NaOH, with the OH- ion accepting this proton to form water, leaving Na+ and Cl- to form sodium chloride. )
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
