568 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 371 0 R
/E (Finally, to find the pH of a substance, we use the formula pH = -log [H+], where the logarithm is base 10. This is because the pH is a logarithmic scale, increasing by 1 every time the concentration of protons decreases by a power of 10.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
569 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 371 0 R
/E (Following this introduction to acids and bases, it soon becomes clear how water fits in with these ideas. It has 2 hydrogens that it could donate to another species, but also has 2 lone pairs of electrons, each ready to accept another proton. This results in its ability to act as an acid, for example when it reacts with ammonia by donating a proton to it, as well as its ability to be a base, for example when it accepts a proton during its reaction with hydrochloric acid. This dual ability is known as amphiproticity, with only a small number of substances having this same property.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
570 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 371 0 R
/T (Autoionization)
/E (Autoionization)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
571 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 371 0 R
/E (As well as being able to react with other species as an acid or base, water can also react with itself in this way, in a process known as autoionisation. This lays the foundations for the pH scale and understanding it, making it significant in not only pH-sensitive biological processes, such as the regulation of blood pH in the human body, but also industrial applications such as water treatment.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
572 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 372 0 R
/T <>
/E <>
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
573 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 372 0 R
/T (This concept helps us to understand why pure water has a pH of exactly 7. In pure water, [H+]=[OH-], and so assigning this value as x results in the expression x2 = 1.00 x 10-14, leading us to find that x = 1.00 x 10-7. Taking x to equal [H+], we can find pH by doing pH= -log[H+], giving -log\(1.00 x 10-7\) = 7. This relationship forms the basis of the pH scale, placing water exactly in the centre between 0 and 14, giving scientists a method for measuring the alkalinity and acidity of substances. The applications of this pH scale are incredibly valuable to a range of industries, in particular when it comes to pharmaceuticals. When developing drugs, pharmacologists need to be aware of the various pH levels in the body and how these can affect drug performance. For example, the pH of the stomach is around 1-2 while the pH of the small intestine lies between 5 and 7, meaning that weakly basic compounds dissolve more easily in the stomach while weakly acidic compounds dissolve more easily in the intestines. This has led scientists to alter the conditions under which certain oral drugs are taken to influence their absorption. An example is the coadministration of Posaconazole, a slightly basic compound taken as a treatment for invasive fungal infections, with a cola beverage of pH 2.5, a the lower pH of the cola increases the solubility of the drug in the stomach, boosting its absorption. )
/E (This concept helps us to understand why pure water has a pH of exactly 7. In pure water, [H+]=[OH-], and so assigning this value as x results in the expression x2 = 1.00 x 10-14, leading us to find that x = 1.00 x 10-7. Taking x to equal [H+], we can find pH by doing pH= -log[H+], giving -log\(1.00 x 10-7\) = 7. This relationship forms the basis of the pH scale, placing water exactly in the centre between 0 and 14, giving scientists a method for measuring the alkalinity and acidity of substances. The applications of this pH scale are incredibly valuable to a range of industries, in particular when it comes to pharmaceuticals. When developing drugs, pharmacologists need to be aware of the various pH levels in the body and how these can affect drug performance. For example, the pH of the stomach is around 1-2 while the pH of the small intestine lies between 5 and 7, meaning that weakly basic compounds dissolve more easily in the stomach while weakly acidic compounds dissolve more easily in the intestines. This has led scientists to alter the conditions under which certain oral drugs are taken to influence their absorption. An example is the coadministration of Posaconazole, a slightly basic compound taken as a treatment for invasive fungal infections, with a cola beverage of pH 2.5, a the lower pH of the cola increases the solubility of the drug in the stomach, boosting its absorption. )
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
574 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 372 0 R
/T (Another important application of pH in pharmaceuticals is understanding protein crystallization, as various pH levels can affect the structure and solubility of proteins. Investigations focused on human insulin found that higher pH levels increased its solubility, an important property that ensures it can move into the blood quickly to manage blood sugar levels. This holds significance when it comes to the use of insulin as a treatment for diabetics, as while short-acting insulin must be soluble so it can be taken quickly before meals to prevent blood glucose levels being too high when carbohydrates are eaten, long-acting insulin should not be as soluble so it can work slowly to keep levels stable between meals, meaning it is important that pharmacologists understand the nuance between the pH values of different forms of insulin.)
/E (Another important application of pH in pharmaceuticals is understanding protein crystallization, as various pH levels can affect the structure and solubility of proteins. Investigations focused on human insulin found that higher pH levels increased its solubility, an important property that ensures it can move into the blood quickly to manage blood sugar levels. This holds significance when it comes to the use of insulin as a treatment for diabetics, as while short-acting insulin must be soluble so it can be taken quickly before meals to prevent blood glucose levels being too high when carbohydrates are eaten, long-acting insulin should not be as soluble so it can work slowly to keep levels stable between meals, meaning it is important that pharmacologists understand the nuance between the pH values of different forms of insulin.)
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [2]
>>
