/T (  The first popular application of this technology is directly derived from the nature of quantum computing. Molecules, atoms and electrons already obey the laws of quantum mechanics, so attempting to simulate them using classical bits can become extraordinarily computationally demanding. A quantum computer instead operates according to those same underlying principles, potentially allowing scientists to model complex molecules, chemical reactions and new materials far more efficiently.)
/E (  The first popular application of this technology is directly derived from the nature of quantum computing. Molecules, atoms and electrons already obey the laws of quantum mechanics, so attempting to simulate them using classical bits can become extraordinarily computationally demanding. A quantum computer instead operates according to those same underlying principles, potentially allowing scientists to model complex molecules, chemical reactions and new materials far more efficiently.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [2]
>>
