734 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 396 0 R
/E (When infinity first starts breaking the rules, however, is when we begin to compare infinite sets with each other. A common method of comparison in set theory involves a process of matching elements in one set with another and checking for a bijection, also known as a one-to-one correspondence between the items in set A and set B. If such a bijection does exist, these sets are of the same size. In set theory, size is widely understood as the cardinality of a set, which describes the number of elements in a set, even when a set is infinite. The process of pairing up items in a list tends to be less algebraically rigorous compared to most modules encountered in high school maths - the skill it actually requires is a deeply imaginative thought process.)
/Pg 53 0 R
/K [19]
>>
endobj
735 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 396 0 R
/T (Comparing infinite sets)
/E (Comparing infinite sets)
/Pg 53 0 R
/K [20]
>>
endobj
736 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 396 0 R
/E (A simple example we can start with to understand what a bijection looks like, and how it can subvert our expectations, is by comparing the set of all integers with the set of natural numbers. )
/Pg 53 0 R
/K [21]
>>
endobj
737 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 396 0 R
/E (Figure 1a: A comparison between the natural numbers \(row 1\) and the integers \(row 2\). Note that there are two definitions of the set of natural numbers, this example includes 0, as this is typically used in set theory, the branch of mathematics we are covering.)
/Pg 53 0 R
/K [22]
>>
endobj
738 0 obj
