739 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 397 0 R
/T (When actually pairing the natural numbers with the integers, it is best to arrange them in such a way that every odd number in the natural numbers is paired with a positive number in the integers, and every even number in the natural numbers is paired with a negative number in the integers. If we count both the set of even natural numbers and the set of odd natural numbers separately, it is clear that both of these are infinite sets. Therefore, the natural numbers can pair with the integers infinitely in both the positive and negative directions. As every natural number appears exactly once, and every integer also appears exactly once, we have constructed a bijection between the two sets, proving they have the same cardinality.)
/E (When actually pairing the natural numbers with the integers, it is best to arrange them in such a way that every odd number in the natural numbers is paired with a positive number in the integers, and every even number in the natural numbers is paired with a negative number in the integers. If we count both the set of even natural numbers and the set of odd natural numbers separately, it is clear that both of these are infinite sets. Therefore, the natural numbers can pair with the integers infinitely in both the positive and negative directions. As every natural number appears exactly once, and every integer also appears exactly once, we have constructed a bijection between the two sets, proving they have the same cardinality.)
/Pg 54 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
740 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 397 0 R
/T (The surprising result we gain from this is that these two sets of numbers are indeed the same size, despite the integers containing both positive and negative numbers, while the natural numbers only include the non-negative numbers. In set theory, infinite lists such as the integers can be referred to as countable, which, by definition, is a set that has a one-to-one correspondence with the natural numbers. Practically, a countably infinite set can typically be identified if its elements can be arranged in a logical sequence, e.g. the prime numbers, as they can be arranged in a sequence in which every prime will eventually appear.)
/E (The surprising result we gain from this is that these two sets of numbers are indeed the same size, despite the integers containing both positive and negative numbers, while the natural numbers only include the non-negative numbers. In set theory, infinite lists such as the integers can be referred to as countable, which, by definition, is a set that has a one-to-one correspondence with the natural numbers. Practically, a countably infinite set can typically be identified if its elements can be arranged in a logical sequence, e.g. the prime numbers, as they can be arranged in a sequence in which every prime will eventually appear.)
/Pg 54 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
