/E (As seen in the figure, by forming a table of decimals, we can visualise a diagonal line such that for the first decimal, the first digit is highlighted, and for the second decimal, the second digit, and so on and so forth. From here, if we want to create a decimal that is unique to the table, we can do so by using a different digit than the one highlighted in the diagonal line. By doing so, the new number differs from the first number in its first digit, the second number in its second digit, the third number in its third digit, and so on to the nth number in its nth digit. For this reason, this number will never be identical to any number on the original list. This proof and the subsequent discovery of uncountably infinite sets has led to one of the most counterintuitive conclusions within mathematics: Some infinities are larger than others.)
/Pg 55 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
