784 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 405 0 R
/E (                 n 2003, an international team of scientists completed a high-quality reference sequence of the human genome after 13 years of work. The Human Genome Project was far more than a single sequencing run: it involved genome mapping, sequencing, development of new technologies, studies of model organisms, and research into the ethical, legal, and social implications of genomics. The project ultimately cost about $2.7 billion in U.S. funding. Today, by contrast, a laboratory can generate a whole-genome sequence in a much shorter time and at a tiny fraction of the cost of the overall Human Genome Project. The comparison therefore illustrates not that one genome once required thirteen years to sequence, but how dramatically sequencing technology, automation, and computational analysis have changed since the project began. It happened because DNA sequencing itself was fundamentally transformed.)
/Pg 62 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
785 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 405 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 62 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
