/K [3]
>>
endobj
787 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 406 0 R
/T (The Development of Sanger Sequencing)
/E (The Development of Sanger Sequencing)
/Pg 63 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
788 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 406 0 R
/E (  Sanger sequencing, developed by Frederick Sanger and his colleagues in 1977, was the first widely successful method for determining the precise sequence of nucleotides in DNA. The technique relies on a process called chain termination. A DNA sample is combined with DNA polymerase, primers, normal nucleotides, and chemically modified nucleotides called dideoxynucleotides \(ddNTPs\). As the polymerase builds new DNA strands, it occasionally incorporates a ddNTP instead of a normal nucleotide. Because ddNTPs lack the chemical group needed to attach the next base, the strand's growth stops at that point. This produces a mixture of DNA fragments of different lengths, each ending in a particular base. By separating these fragments according to size and determining their terminal bases, scientists can reconstruct the DNA sequence. The method was foundational to modern genomics and was ultimately used, with substantial technological improvements, during the Human Genome Project.)
/Pg 63 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
789 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 406 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 63 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
790 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 406 0 R
/T (The Rise of Next-Generation Sequencing)
/E (The Rise of Next-Generation Sequencing)
/Pg 63 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
791 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 406 0 R
/E (  Next-generation sequencing fundamentally reimagined how sequencing could be done. Rather than processing DNA fragments sequentially, short-read NGS platforms sequence enormous numbers of fragments in parallel through a process called massively parallel sequencing. Illumina's sequencing-by-synthesis approach, for example, uses DNA fragments attached to a flow cell, bridge amplification to create clusters, and fluorescent reversible-terminator nucleotides to determine the sequence as bases are incorporated. A landmark 2008 study demonstrated that this massively parallel approach could generate billions of bases in a single experiment and apply the technology to whole-human-genome sequencing.)
/Pg 63 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
792 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 407 0 R
/E (  The differences between the Human Genome Project and modern sequencing need to be framed carefully. The Human Genome Project took about 13 years and cost approximately $2.7 billion in total U.S. funding, but that figure covered a broad scientific program rather than a single sequencing run. Modern sequencing instruments can generate enormous quantities of sequence data in hours or days, while the cost of sequencing an individual genome has fallen dramatically. NHGRI describes this broader technological progress as involving roughly a million-fold reduction in the cost of sequencing a human genome since the beginning of the field. NGS also offers far greater scalability, allowing researchers to sequence entire genomes, exomes, or targeted gene panels for many samples in a single run rather than one gene or fragment at a time. This combination of speed, affordability, and throughput transformed sequencing from a specialized, resource-intensive endeavor into a routine laboratory and clinical tool.)
/Pg 64 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
793 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 407 0 R
/T (Transforming Biology and Medicine)
/E (Transforming Biology and Medicine)
/Pg 64 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
794 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 407 0 R
/E (  The scale and affordability of NGS have driven transformative changes across biology and medicine. In cancer research, NGS allows scientists to sequence a tumor's DNA and compare it to a patient's normal tissue, revealing mutations associated with an individual's cancer. This has enabled the identification of actionable mutations, such as alterations in EGFR or BRCA-related genes, that can help guide targeted therapies rather than relying entirely on one-size-fits-all treatment.)
/Pg 64 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
795 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 407 0 R
/E (  In rare disease diagnosis, NGS has been especially powerful. Many rare genetic disorders previously went undiagnosed for years because testing one gene at a time was impractical. Whole-exome and whole-genome sequencing now allow clinicians to examine a patient's protein-coding regions or entire genome at once, helping shorten the diagnostic process for many families.)
/Pg 64 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
796 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 407 0 R
/E (NGS has also become central to precision medicine, an approach that uses genetic information to help tailor treatment decisions to individual patients. In infectious disease surveillance, NGS proved essential during the COVID-19 pandemic: public health agencies such as the CDC used whole-genome sequencing of viral samples to identify SARS-CoV-2 variants and track their spread, informing public health responses. Beyond medicine, NGS has expanded genomic research broadly, enabling large-scale studies of population genetics, microbiomes, and evolutionary biology that would have been difficult or impossible with earlier sequencing approaches.)
/Pg 64 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
797 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 408 0 R
/T (The Future of DNA Sequencing)
/E (The Future of DNA Sequencing)
/Pg 65 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
798 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 408 0 R
/E (  DNA sequencing continues to evolve beyond conventional short-read NGS. Long-read sequencing technologies, including those developed by Pacific Biosciences and Oxford Nanopore, can read DNA fragments thousands to hundreds of thousands of bases long. Because of their distinctive read lengths and single-molecule approaches, long-read platforms are commonly classified as a distinct third-generation sequencing \(TGS\) approach rather than simply being treated as another form of standard short-read NGS. Their long reads are particularly useful for resolving repetitive and structurally complex regions of the genome that short-read sequencing can struggle to assemble.)
/Pg 65 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
799 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 408 0 R
/E (  This distinction also helps explain why long-read sequencing represents more than simply a faster version of NGS. Short-read sequencing revolutionized genomics through massive parallelization, while third-generation long-read methods introduced different strategies for reading DNA molecules and addressing limitations caused by short reads. These technologies have contributed to the completion of previously difficult regions of the human genome, including repetitive regions that could not be fully resolved during the original Human Genome Project.)
/Pg 65 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
800 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 408 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 65 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
801 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 408 0 R
/E (  Still, significant challenges remain. Sequencing technology is not equally accessible worldwide, and disparities in access to genomic testing risk widening existing health inequities. The sheer volume of genomic data being generated also outpaces our ability to interpret it fully; many genetic variants remain of uncertain significance because their biological effects are not yet understood. Finally, the growing use of genomic data raises pressing ethical questions around privacy, consent, and potential genetic discrimination, all of which society will need to address as sequencing becomes further embedded in healthcare.)
/Pg 65 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
