/P 411 0 R
/E (S)
/Pg 68 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
814 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 412 0 R
/E (  This development created a major shift in how scientists understand sleep. Instead of treating sleep as one continuous block of time, researchers began examining the different changes that occur throughout the night. Sleep consists of non-REM and REM stages, which have different patterns of brain and body activity. This helped scientists understand that sleep is an active process rather than simply a period when the body and mind shut down to rest. Before these advances in sleep science, people generally viewed sleep as a simple period of rest and had much less understanding of what was happening internally. Modern methods such as polysomnography have allowed researchers to study these changes in much greater detail \(Smith et al.\))
/Pg 69 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
815 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 412 0 R
/Pg 69 0 R
/A [1647 0 R 0]
/K [1]
>>
endobj
816 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 412 0 R
/T (From Alarm Clocks to Measuring Sleep)
/E (From Alarm Clocks to Measuring Sleep)
/Pg 69 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
817 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 412 0 R
/E (  To begin, traditional alarm clocks were mainly designed to control when people woke up, but they did not provide information about what happened during the night. People could know when they went to bed and when they woke up, but they had little information about the different changes happening while they were asleep. As sleep science developed, researchers began using more detailed methods to understand what happens during sleep. One example is polysomnography \(PSG\), which records brain activity along with other signals from the body to identify sleep and wake periods. Smith et al. explain that polysomnography is an important clinical method for evaluating sleep because it provides much more detailed information than simply observing whether someone appears to be asleep.)
/Pg 69 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
818 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 413 0 R
/E (  The modern state of wearable technology has changed how people measure their sleep by making it possible to collect sleep information from home rather than only in a sleep laboratory. Because devices such as smartwatches, fitness bands, and smart rings use sensors like accelerometers, heart rate monitors, and other physiological measurements to estimate when a person is asleep and identify general sleep patterns. According to Smith et al., actigraphy provides an objective way to monitor sleep over extended periods in a person's normal environment, making it a practical tool for observing everyday sleep habits rather than relying only on clinical testing.)
/Pg 70 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
819 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 413 0 R
/E (  Subsequently, an additional advantage of wearable technology is that it allows people to review their sleep data over time instead of relying only on their memory. Rather than trying to remember how well they slept, users can compare information from multiple nights to identify trends in their sleep schedule. As Yuan et al. explain, while actigraphy has limitations in identifying specific sleep stages, it is still useful for tracking overall sleep patterns across extended periods.)
/Pg 70 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
820 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 413 0 R
/E (  A real-world example of this can be seen through a high school student monitoring their sleep schedule. In the past, a student might have only used an alarm clock to wake up for school. Today, that same student could wear a smartwatch every night and review several weeks of sleep data to notice patterns in when they go to bed, wake up, or get less sleep before busy school days. Although the exact sleep-stage estimates should not be treated as a medical diagnosis, the device can still provide valuable insight into overall sleep habits and encourage healthier sleep routines \(Smith et al.; Yuan et al.\).)
/Pg 70 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
821 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 413 0 R
/Pg 70 0 R
/A [1648 0 R 0]
/K [3]
>>
endobj
822 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 413 0 R
/T (The Rise of Sleep Trackers)
/E (The Rise of Sleep Trackers)
/Pg 70 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
823 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 414 0 R
/T (How Accurate Are Sleep Trackers, And Why Does Sleep Data Matter?)
/E (How Accurate Are Sleep Trackers, And Why Does Sleep Data Matter?)
/Pg 71 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
824 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 414 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 71 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
825 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 414 0 R
/E (  Wearable sleep trackers generally estimate sleep using movement and other sensor data. This can make them useful for identifying broad sleep patterns, but they have more difficulty determining exactly which sleep stage someone is in. Smith et al. explain that actigraphy can be useful for measuring sleep and wake patterns, but it has limitations compared with more comprehensive sleep measurements. More recently, Yuan et al. reviewed studies comparing wrist-worn actigraphy with polysomnography and found that actigraphy had some ability to classify different sleep stages, but the studies varied considerably in how they grouped sleep stages and measured accuracy. )
/Pg 71 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
826 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 414 0 R
/E (  This shows that modern technology can give people more information about their sleep, but having more information does not necessarily mean that the information is perfect. For example, a sleep tracker might show a pattern of shorter sleep over several weeks, which could help someone recognize their general sleep habits. However, its estimated amounts of REM, deep, or light sleep should be treated as estimates rather than a medical diagnosis. The research on actigraphy shows that these devices can be useful for observing general sleep patterns while still having limitations when measuring specific aspects of sleep.)
/Pg 71 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
