3401 0 obj
<<
/Title (This concept helps us to understand why pure water has a pH of exactly 7. In pure water, [H+]=[OH-], and so assigning this value as x results in the expression x2 = 1.00 x 10-14, leading us to find that x = 1.00 x 10-7. Taking x to equal [H+], we can find pH by doing pH= -log[H+], giving -log\(1.00 x 10-7\) = 7. This relationship forms the basis of the pH scale, placing water exactly in the centre between 0 and 14, giving scientists a method for measuring the alkalinity and acidity of substances. The applications of this pH scale are incredibly valuable to a range of industries, in particular when it comes to pharmaceuticals. When developing drugs, pharmacologists need to be aware of the various pH levels in the body and how these can affect drug performance. For example, the pH of the stomach is around 1-2 while the pH of the small intestine lies between 5 and 7, meaning that weakly basic compounds dissolve more easily in the stomach while weakly acidic compounds dissolve more easily in the intestines. This has led scientists to alter the conditions under which certain oral drugs are taken to influence their absorption. An example is the coadministration of Posaconazole, a slightly basic compound taken as a treatment for invasive fungal infections, with a cola beverage of pH 2.5, a the lower pH of the cola increases the solubility of the drug in the stomach, boosting its absorption.)
/Dest [29 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 3399 0 R
/Next 3403 0 R
>>
