3441 0 obj
<<
/Title (The surprising result we gain from this is that these two sets of numbers are indeed the same size, despite the integers containing both positive and negative numbers, while the natural numbers only include the non-negative numbers. In set theory, infinite lists such as the integers can be referred to as countable, which, by definition, is a set that has a one-to-one correspondence with the natural numbers. Practically, a countably infinite set can typically be identified if its elements can be arranged in a logical sequence, e.g. the prime numbers, as they can be arranged in a sequence in which every prime will eventually appear.)
/Dest [54 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 3443 0 R
/Next 3439 0 R
>>
endobj
3442 0 obj
<<
